## Core model sequence (OLS, standardized DV; S1 n=9,310)
| Term | A: demographics | B: +SES | C: +ASVAB | change B→C |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Black | −0.267*** | −0.018 (ns) | +0.050** | reversal |
| Hispanic | −0.082*** | +0.074*** | +0.249*** | grows positive |
| SES z-index (per SD) | — | 0.371*** | 0.166*** | **−55.3%** |
| ASVAB composite (per SD) | — | — | 0.282*** | — |
| R² | 0.595 | 0.640 | 0.689 | ASVAB block F=1469 (p≈0) |

*(R² high because outcome is years of schooling completed by a 14–22yo cohort and age mechanically predicts grade line; age is controlled in every model, so block comparisons are valid.)*

## Individual SES indicators (S_ind n=5,531) — % change B→C
linc (family income) **+84.4%** attenuation; father's edu +54.3%; pro/mgr occupation +64.8%; mother's edu +36.5%; family size → ≈0 (+113%). Parental-education and income effects shrink most when ASVAB is added.

## Class-rank subsample (S2 n=4,546; restricted)
Class standing adds a small but significant increment (b=0.148, p=1.5e-8); SESz attenuation Bz→D ≈ 51% (≈55% with ASVAB alone) → school-performance rank adds little beyond ASVAB.

## Logit (some college, ≥13 yrs; S1)
Black OR 1.57→**2.74**, Hispanic OR 1.45→**4.23** after adding ASVAB; ASVAB OR≈3.98 per SD; SESz OR 4.5→2.2. ≥16 "BA proxy": only n=39 total (Black 5, Hispanic 4, White 28) — too sparse, reported only.

## Mediation via ASVAB (product method, 500-bootstrap CI)
| Variable | total | indirect (via ASVAB) | direct | % mediated |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| SESz | 0.371 | 0.240 [0.226, 0.256] | 0.131 | **64.7%** |
| Black | −0.018 | −0.136 [−0.152, −0.121] | +0.119 | suppression* |
| Hispanic | +0.074 | −0.219 [−0.236, −0.205] | +0.294 | suppression* |

*(Race: indirect is negative because minority → lower ASVAB → less schooling; direct is positive because, net of ability, minority respondents get more schooling — the "sign-opposite indirect" is classic suppression, not attenuation.)*

## Moderation (interactions, S1)
SESz×ASVAB = −0.079 (p<0.001): SES slope 0.265 at low ability → 0.058 at high ability (SES matters most for low-readiness students).
Hispanic×ASVAB = −0.044 (p=0.012): Hispanic advantage narrows with ability (0.28→0.16).
Black×ASVAB = +0.057 (p=0.003): Black advantage widens with ability (0.01→0.16).

## Missing-data sensitivity (20 imputations, chained OLS, Rubin's rules; n=12,686)
Pooled Bz→Cz: SESz 0.403→0.180 (**−55.4%**), Black −0.040→+0.039, Hispanic +0.072→+0.272 — same pattern/magnitudes as complete-case.

## Final natural-language hypothesis (data-grounded)
"Considering academic characteristics substantially attenuates the estimated effect of family socioeconomic status on educational attainment and reverses the sign of race effects. Without academic characteristics, a 1-SD increase in an SES index (family income, parental education, father's occupation, family size) is associated with ~0.37 SD higher schooling; after adding ASVAB scores this drops to ~0.17 SD (55% attenuation; family income, father's education, and occupation each attenuate 54–84%), meaning the majority of the SES gradient in schooling operates through academic readiness, with roughly 65% of the total SES association mediated by test scores. For race, the raw Black disadvantage (−0.27 SD, largely reflecting lower family SES) disappears once SES is controlled (−0.02, ns); after also conditioning on academic characteristics, both Black (+0.05 SD) and Hispanic (+0.25 SD) respondents complete significantly MORE schooling than comparable non-Hispanic white peers — an 'over-performance relative to predicted ability' pattern that is strongest in the probability of any college (OR ≈ 2.7–4.2). The SES gradient is steep among low-ability students and near zero among high-ability students, while minority advantages persist across the ability distribution (and widen with ability for Black students). Caveats: outcome is early attainment (cohort aged 14–22 in 1979), no direct BA-completion measure exists (BA proxy n=39, unanalyzable), 1981 ASVAB post-dates the 1979 outcome (associational, not causal), and the class-rank analysis is restricted to respondents in school in 1980."
